<?xml version="1.0"?>
<rdf:RDF xmlns="http://www.example.org/muac/ontology/local-moo#"
     xml:base="http://www.example.org/muac/ontology/local-moo"
     xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"
     xmlns:owl="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#"
     xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
     xmlns:xml="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace"
     xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#"
     xmlns:rdfs="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"
     xmlns:skos="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#"
     xmlns:local-moo="http://www.example.org/muac/ontology/local-moo#">
    <owl:Ontology rdf:about="http://www.example.org/muac/ontology/local-moo">
        <rdfs:comment>Convention for naming relations (Object Properties)

1. The Active-Passive Pattern (most relations)
* Forward Relation: Active verb in the present tense (e.g., employs, teaches, owns).
  - Points from the independent entity (Whole), active agent (Actor), or creator to the   dependent entity (Part), passive object (Target), or creation. 
* Reverse Relation: Passive voice or reciprocal verb (e.g., is_employed_by, is_taught_by, is_owned_by).
Example:
 - Forward: Organization employs Person
 - Reverse: Person is_employed_by Organization

2. The Has-Is-Of Pattern (hiearchical relations)
* Forward Relation: Starts with has_ to indicate ownership, composition, or property.
* Reverse Relation: Starts with is_ or ends with _of to point back to the subject.
Example:
 - Forward: Car has_part Engine
 - Reverse: Engine is_part_of Car

Determines property direction based on priority: Forward relations point from the independent entity (Whole), active agent (Actor), or creator to the dependent entity (Part), passive object (Target), or creation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>TODO:
- Capabilities are realized by Flows
- Function Exchanges are allocated to Structure Exchanges</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Ontology>
    


